# 3. Language and dialect detection

## Do not force one language before transcription

For mixed Lebanese speech, use an ASR configuration that supports automatic language detection or a language set containing:

```text
ar-LB or Arabic
English
French
```

Arabizi is not a formal speech language code. It is usually spoken Arabic, while the resulting transcript may contain Latin-script words and numbers. The normalizer must handle both.

## Store language evidence

Save:

```text
primary_language
secondary_languages
script_mix
arabizi_detected
language_confidence
segment_languages
```

Example:

```json
{
  "primary_language": "ar-LB",
  "secondary_languages": ["en", "fr"],
  "script_mix": ["arabic", "latin"],
  "arabizi_detected": true,
  "language_confidence": 0.88,
  "segments": [
    {"start": 0.0, "end": 2.2, "language": "ar-LB"},
    {"start": 2.2, "end": 4.1, "language": "en"}
  ]
}
```

## Lebanese speech patterns to support

The normalizer should recognize variations such as:

```text
بدي / بديّي / بدّي / bade / badde
تنين / تنتين / tnen / tntein / etnein
شاورما / shawarma / shawrma / shawerma
من / men / mn
مع / ma3 / ma'a
بدون / bala / bala ma3
زائد / extra / zeyede
ما بدي / ma bade / no
واحد / one / un / wa7ad
```

These are examples, not a closed dictionary. Build aliases from real, consented, corrected customer transcripts.

## Do not “correct” names too aggressively

The system must preserve:

```text
raw_transcript
normalized_transcript
customer_confirmed_transcript
```

A dish name should be resolved against the vendor’s live menu, not changed solely by a language model.

