# 7. Voice note types

## Type A — simple informational request

> “What’s good tonight?”

May use one model call or deterministic recommendations. No transcript confirmation is necessary if no order action follows, but show a short interpreted response when the meaning is uncertain.

## Type B — menu search

> “Do you have chicken shawarma?”

Transcribe, search the current menu, show item cards. No order is created.

## Type C — basket request

> “I want two burgers and one fries.”

Transcript confirmation, item resolution, basket card, final order confirmation.

## Type D — address or location

> “Deliver it near the church beside the blue pharmacy.”

Extract a draft address only. Ask the customer to confirm the village and landmark. Prefer a shared location pin when the address is ambiguous.

## Type E — cancellation or order change

> “Cancel my order.”

Do not execute based on transcription alone. Show the recognized order and a cancellation confirmation button. Check the current order state.

## Type F — payment or refund request

> “Refund my money” or “I paid already.”

Route to a safe support/payment flow. Never let voice directly issue a refund or mark payment as complete.

## Type G — allergy or medical concern

> “I’m allergic to nuts.”

Store only with the correct consent and profile policy. Ask the customer to confirm the allergy preference and tell them Alo King cannot guarantee kitchen safety unless the merchant confirms it. Escalate where needed.

## Type H — merchant voice note

Merchant voice notes require stricter authorization and a confirmation summary:

> “I heard: mark chicken shawarma sold out until 8 PM and pause orders for 30 minutes. Apply these changes?

Buttons:

```text
Apply changes
Edit
Cancel
```

Never execute multiple merchant mutations from an unconfirmed voice note.

